 ## Overview
-Parallel Image Assembly is the final stage of tiled parallel volume rendering where per-process or per-rank RGBA sub-images are collected and blended into a single final image [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of reconstructing a coherent global view from disjoint spatial tiles processed independently [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike its siblings which handle tile distribution or boundary computation, this method focuses on the global accumulation of color and opacity, typically using front-to-back alpha compositing [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Parallel Image Assembly assembles per-process RGBA sub-images into a final image by placing them in their spatial coordinates and blending along the z-axis [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of reconstructing a coherent global view from disjoint spatial tiles processed independently [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike its siblings which handle tile distribution or boundary computation, this method focuses on the global accumulation of color and opacity [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
